<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Copán</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oxwitik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Copán is an archaeological site of the Maya civilization in the Copán <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of western Honduras, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Occupied for more than two thousand years, it was the capital of a major Classic period kingdom from the 5th to the 9th century CE, refounded in 426 by a dynasty with its origins at Tikal; at its Late Classic peak the kingdom had at least 20,000 people. In 738 its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waxaklajuun-ubaah-kawiil">Uaxaclajuun Ub'aah K'awiil</a> was captured and beheaded by his former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quirigua">Quiriguá</a>. The population later declined and the city was abandoned in the early 10th century. Known for its sculpture and Hieroglyphic Stairway, it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copan.jpg" alt="Copán" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nosferattus · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copán_Stela_B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In culture, the country preserves the Maya city of <b>Copán</b>, inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1980 and famous for its sculpted stelae and the Hieroglyphic Stairway, the longest known Maya inscription.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Maya cities, links to Teotihuacan became a source of prestige: K’inich Yax K’uk’ Mo’, who founded the royal dynasty of <b>Copán</b> in 426, was later portrayed with Teotihuacan-style goggles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Rulers raided rival cities to take captives, who were displayed, humiliated and often sacrificed, and the greatest wars could end reigns: in 738 Waxaklajuun Ub’aah K’awiil, king of <b>Copán</b>, was captured and executed by his former vassal, the king of Quiriguá.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Classic Maya lowlands (c. 250–900 CE) dozens of cities such as Tikal, Calakmul, <b>Copán</b> and Palenque were ruled by holy lords who recorded their wars and alliances in inscriptions; Tikal and Calakmul led rival networks of allies and vassals, and in 562 Tikal was defeated by…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Archaeology of houses and burials, as at <b>Copán</b> and the buried village of Joya de Cerén, shows how ordinary people lived.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li><li><blockquote>Quiriguá was founded or refounded in 426 CE as a dependency of Copán, about 50 kilometres to the south, by agents of <b>Copán</b>'s founder K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo'.</blockquote><a href="/state/quirigua">Quiriguá</a></li></ul></section>
